WEBVTT

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:28.150
<b>LEGEND</b>

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:28.150
<b>OF THE</b>

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:28.150
<b>  GALACTIC HEROES</b>

00:01:53.730 --> 00:02:02.150
The Retriever
Chapter II

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:07.350
In December of 
Imperial Year 483...

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:11.600
...the cruiser, Hershrichlenchen, under the
command of Commander Reinhard von Musel...

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.030
...independently infiltrated Alliance territory on a top secret mission.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:20.550
Very little is known about the 
navigation routes inside rebel territory.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:27.320
We've only obtained minimal information on these routes around the Iserlohn and Phezzan Corridors.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.380
Normally, there isn't much ship traffic on
the route connecting Iserlohn and Phezzan...

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:36.230
...and there isn't a relay planet, either.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:39.900
Though there are some frontier
patrol fleets assigned to this area...

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.620
...their numbers are rather limited in
relation to the vast area they must cover...

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:47.800
...so we may safely assume that there's little chance for a lone cruiser to be detected by them.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.030
The problem is when we come close to the Phezzan Corridor.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.550
It may not be as heavily
patrolled as Iserlohn...

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:57.660
...but there are rebel border
patrol units stationed there.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.750
Are these pieces of information coming from Phezzan ?

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:03.640
They're from the
Imperial Military Liaison Officer.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.700
The merchants of Phezzan have no qualms about selling their parents...

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.580
...but they never sell 
navigation route information.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:19.900
Anyway, the information was gathered by the
Liaison Officer and others over a long period of time.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.000
I believe it is quite credible.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:30.440
In this mission we're supposed to be able to receive intelligence support from the Military Liaison Officer.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.860
Unless we trust this information,
we can't do anything.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.450
We shouldn't put too
much confidence in it...

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.650
...but still, we have no choice but to use it as the basis{of our actions}.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:40.470
You're right.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:44.840
While remaining on alert, we'll take the shortest route to Phezzan.

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:45.900
Yes, sir.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:47.060
Very well. Dismissed.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:56.310
At this point, the Alliance was not aware 
of the infiltration by the Hershrichlenchen...

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:58.670
...so in that regard, there
were not many difficulties.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:04.410
The cruiser encountered a patrol unit once but escaped harm by blending in with a natural object...

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.000
...and with only a one day delay
from the initial schedule...

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.680
...successfully reached the space area near Phezzan Corridor.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.370
We've reached the 
predetermined coordinates.

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:18.220
Shut down the engine.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:19.070
Shutting down the engine.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:28.850
We'll be on standby here awaiting communications from the Imperial Military Liaison Officer at Phezzan.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:31.900
They're keeping an
eye on Helksheimer...

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:35.510
...and they're supposed to contact us as soon as he leaves Phezzan.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:38.750
We've received
a coded message.

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:41.480
Isn't the timing{of the contact} too good ?

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Not good.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:59.000
It seems Helksheimer's ship already left Phezzan yesterday, at 09:00.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.250
They've also sent
information on the ship.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:03.400
And there's a single escort ship.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.900
Chief Navigator, calculate
their estimated coordinates.{of Helksheimer's ship}

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:07.700
Yes, sir.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:16.690
Most likely, they{Helksheimer's ship} exited the Corridor at least three or four hours ago, in my estimation.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:22.710
We have the estimated coordinates.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:30.520
The one day delay we suffered when we had to hide from the enemy was costly.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:33.800
What will we do ?

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.640
We'll follow them immediately.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:40.050
We couldn't get them before they left, but we can still catch them.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:41.850
No, we will catch them.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:42.550
Yes, sir.

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:55.900
But they really did contact
us with such good timing.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:58.450
Since they didn't know our ETA...

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:03.110
...they must have continually sent the
codes to the predetermined coordinates.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:06.330
Ultra-high speed communications are highly directional.

00:06:06.330 --> 00:06:11.330
Plus, since it's coded, they must have decided that the possibility of interception was low.

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:14.100
But still, how ?

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:20.330
The Military Liaison Officer is supposed to come out near the Corridor's exit on an intelligence vessel.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:23.290
From that location, they will 
support us by sending information...

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:30.770
...while also creating some sort of interference so
that other ships can't leave the Corridor for a while.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.400
That's helpful, but it
really is a risky mission.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:36.900
I mean, it's risky not so 
much in terms of our lives...

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.550
...but rather in terms of
the political implications.

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:43.250
In the unlikely event where that action is
detected by Phezzan or the rebel forces...

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:48.990
The importance of this retrieval operation
outweighs such risks - that must be what it is.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:06:58.900
Can we trace the ship's path ?

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:02.800
We're conducting a density analysis based on five-dimensional data...

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:07.290
...and there are approximately 18 ships that exited the Corridor in the corresponding time frame.

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:10.850
From among them, exclude ships that are sailing alone.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.100
Six groups are left,
13 ships in total.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:18.900
Which group has a
party of three ships ?

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:19.660
That's this one.

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:21.750
Okay, eliminate that one, too.

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.150
Five groups are left,
10 ships in total.

00:07:28.130 --> 00:07:30.050
No. 1, which one
do you think it is ?

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:34.670
I would think it's the slowest pair.

00:07:34.670 --> 00:07:36.030
Your reason ?

00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:40.230
Aside from the target ships, I would assume all the others are Phezzani merchant ships.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:43.250
Since they're carrying merchandise...

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:46.580
...they would take the shortest
routes at maximum efficiency.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:50.600
On the other hand, Helksheimer no longer has a reason to hurry.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:52.970
No, that's not necessarily the case.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:54.840
They are defectors.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.250
They're impatiently trying to secure the protection of the Alliance government...

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:00.900
...um, well...

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:04.650
...of the rebels, 
as soon as possible.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:07.430
We should be suspicious
of the speediest pair !

00:08:07.430 --> 00:08:09.150
What do you think, LTJG Kircheis ?

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:12.050
I agree with the First Officer.

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:14.210
Having gotten through the Corridor...

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:17.210
...they must be thinking that they have secured their own safety.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:21.350
Therefore, precisely because they hurried all the way to Phezzan...

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:23.700
...now that they're safe, 
they'll want to take their time.

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:26.910
That's what I think, too.

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:27.800
Captain !

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.100
Helksheimer is an arrogant man.

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:35.590
When such a man gets flustered, and
stumblingly escapes into the Phezzan Corridor...

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:39.790
...he would at least want to restore some pretense of dignity when he departs.

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:44.320
And by making the Alliance government wait for the souvenir, he can also jack up its price...

00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:48.390
...and he'll also try to increase his value as a defector accordingly.

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.180
We designate this slowest
ship to be our target.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:54.900
We'll pursue the target
at maximum speed.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:55.350
Yes, sir.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:03.540
There's a ship in front of us.

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.530
It's not the target ship.

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:09.620
It appears to be a tanker-type 
merchant ship heading toward Phezzan.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:11.900
This isn't good.

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:15.350
If they detect our 
presence and report it...

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:16.340
We'll take a detour.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:18.550
We'll use the radar permeation system...

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:22.820
...but we'll also change our route just in case, avoiding their radar range.

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:26.450
But it'll take us more
time to catch the target...

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:30.520
...and that means we'll go
deeper into rebel territory.

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:31.650
That can't be helped.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:35.070
But we have no navigation
data from this point on.

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:36.680
If we get too deep
into enemy territory...

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:37.400
Silence !

00:09:39.180 --> 00:09:45.240
Wasn't it you who said just now that this mission must be carried out despite the risks ?

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:48.620
Besides, I've declared that you have no say over our operational plans.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:49.500
Did you forget ?

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:01.400
Captain, I was intercepting communications...

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:04.790
...but it seems there is an inhabited 
planet ahead on this navigation route.

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:06.150
What's the distance ?

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:10.450
It cannot be measured accurately, but judging from the time lag of normal radio waves...

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:14.180
...it is certain that at cruising speed, it is within one day's travel.

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:16.380
What's the time distance to the target ?

00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:17.150
Approximately three hours.

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:24.400
Patrol fleets should be stationed on an inhabited planet.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:29.280
If we were to capture them before we reach their patrol area, we'll just barely make it.

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:35.440
Moreover, when we reach the target, they'll send a rescue signal.

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:40.810
If we cannot prevent that, it means our presence will be known at that point.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:46.330
It'll be troublesome if enemy patrol fleets are in close proximity.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.450
No. 1, prepare fighters.

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.700
Yes, sir. But...

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:58.150
We'll launch a surprise attack.

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:02.900
We'll make pinpoint attacks,
destroying their communications...

00:11:02.900 --> 00:11:06.130
...and cannons with our fighters while we jam their radar.

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:11.450
At the same time, this ship will destroy the armed
escort ship, immediately close in on the target...

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:14.400
...forcibly bridge onto the target to send soldiers on board for close combat...

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:16.380
...and take over control of 
the ship from the inside.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:21.850
Afterwards, we'll leave this space area before enemy patrol fleets can arrive.

00:11:21.850 --> 00:11:23.170
Order acknowledged.

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:29.070
LTJG Kircheis, lead the assault unit.

00:11:29.070 --> 00:11:31.200
Take over control of the ship...

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:35.620
...and secure the person of Helksheimer as well as the target item.

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:36.600
Yes, sir.

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.800
Time{it takes to execute the plan} is the key here.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:40.940
Each of you, be sure to
confirm the order of our plans.

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:41.860
Yes, sir !

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:50.350
Signs of a ship have been confirmed.

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:51.700
It's definitely the target ship !

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:52.910
Maximum speed !

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:54.900
Jamming output at maximum, also !

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:56.160
Launch Valkyries !

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.550
Each unit commence 
jamming upon departure !

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:28.150
Primary targets have been destroyed !

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:28.900
Good.

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:29.700
The escort is coming about !

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:35.020
Main cannon, fire !

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:41.860
The escort has been destroyed !

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:42.470
Good.

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:44.040
Keep this course and advance.

00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:08.410
Charge !

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:15.700
We're rushing in{to the enemy ship}.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:18.260
Don't neglect to keep
vigilance over the area.

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:21.000
Even though we've shut down their
communications and are jamming the radar...

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:23.600
...the explosion of the escort might have been detected.

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:27.590
Our radar cannot be used either...

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:30.450
Spread the Valkyries out
and keep them on alert.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:31.350
Yes, sir.

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:32.650
Prepare to send a visual signal !

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:55.500
Where is the Count ?

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:57.500
He's in the suite on the top floor...

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:01.830
Group #2, head to the engine room.

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:04.090
Group #3, investigate the cargo hold.{hatch.}

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:05.650
Everyone else, follow me.

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:14.530
Group #4, head to the bridge.

00:14:14.530 --> 00:14:16.640
The rest of you, look for Helksheimer.

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:45.030
Don't move !

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:50.900
Stand up slowly with your
hands behind your head.

00:14:54.230 --> 00:14:56.960
We've successfully seized the bridge and the engine room.

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:58.450
We've taken over
control of the ship.

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:03.380
Also, we've discovered cargo that
appears to be the target in the cargo hold.

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:04.710
We're in the process of confirmation.

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:07.210
What about Helksheimer ?

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:08.430
We're still searching.

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:16.350
Captain, I'd like to move over to that ship and identify the cargo.

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:20.390
You may do so.

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:39.710
Search for him !

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:49.110
Use the infrared radar device.

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:52.500
There may be hidden doors,
investigate the walls as well.

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:53.350
Yes, sir.

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:01.430
We've received a visual
signal from Valkyrie unit 6 !

00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:04.200
An enemy patrol unit is
approaching us from 11 o'clock !

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:06.240
Just as we thought...

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:07.510
Captain.

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:10.230
That ship can move, can't it ?

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:10.950
Yes, sir.

00:16:11.530 --> 00:16:12.220
All right.

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:13.900
Cancel the communication blockade.

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:15.860
Retrieve all Valkyrie units.

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:18.350
Cut the anchors and allow
the ship to sail on its own.

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:25.940
Sir !

00:16:47.020 --> 00:16:48.030
Hey.

00:16:49.590 --> 00:16:51.160
Let's pull it !

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:09.750
It seems it was depressurized from the inside.

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:12.760
The door itself must be an air-lock.

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:13.570
Let's go.

00:17:17.950 --> 00:17:22.000
Radar confirms several approaching
ships in the 11 o'clock direction !

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:24.350
They are presumed to be the reported enemy patrol unit !

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:26.150
Hurry up and prepare to retreat.

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:37.370
They haven't been ejected...?

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:44.160
What happened ?

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:45.210
He's dead.

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:53.680
The depressurization must have failed...

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:55.910
...as they hurried to evacuate...

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:00.670
One's alive !

00:18:01.780 --> 00:18:03.020
This pod is secure !

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:11.950
This is...

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:14.350
The enemy ships are
still approaching !

00:18:17.160 --> 00:18:17.960
They're ready.

00:18:18.560 --> 00:18:19.440
Come about !

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:30.620
Helksheimer is dead ?

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:34.650
It seems there was a decompression accident
while attempting to evacuate in an escape pod.

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:40.550
This result leaves a bad
aftertaste, doesn't it ?

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:43.150
It's too early to discuss any "result."

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:45.400
Our hardships are just beginning.

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:47.250
Returning is more difficult than coming in.

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:52.650
Up to this point, they were
unaware of our presence...

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:57.200
...but now, we will certainly be pursued,
and there may be ambushes, too.

00:18:57.200 --> 00:18:59.040
That's right.

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:00.260
Enemy status ?

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:01.440
Still advancing toward us !

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:04.350
We'll withdraw at maximum battle speed !

00:19:04.350 --> 00:19:09.160
We want to leave this space area at all costs before the enemy recognizes the situation !

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:11.380
Can the other ship keep up with us ?

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:16.450
It should be okay, but since we destroyed its communications and weapons...

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:18.450
...a safe journey might
be difficult to hope for.

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:23.350
If possible, it's better to collect the cargo in question and recall our personnel...

00:19:23.950 --> 00:19:25.350
We can't do that right now.

00:19:25.350 --> 00:19:28.400
We'll think about it after we gain 
enough distance from the enemy.

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:31.750
Have LTJG Kircheis continue
in command of that ship.

00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:34.800
What is it ?

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:36.260
Are you dissatisfied ?

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:38.340
No, I'm fine with it.

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:42.250
But Lt. Commander Bendling
is on that ship, so...

00:19:45.320 --> 00:19:49.060
We'll have the Lt. Commander
concentrate on identifying the target.

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:51.650
Removing it would be a difficult task.

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:55.400
If he has any complaints, I'll talk to him.

00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:56.550
I understand.

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:02.690
The Alliance patrol unit detected the explosion and rushed to the scene, but at this point...

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:07.090
...they had no idea that an Imperial ship had infiltrated their territory.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:11.250
They would only discover the fact that there
was an attack after they arrive at the scene...

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:15.450
...search the wreckage of the destroyed escort ship and analyze the circumstances of the occurrence.

00:20:15.450 --> 00:20:19.860
Even then, they may still consider it an action by space pirates.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:22.250
Only after thoroughly
investigating the debris...

00:20:22.250 --> 00:20:25.600
...will they recognize the
presence of an Imperial ship.

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:28.050
But until then, there
would still be some time.

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:30.500
And in that span of time, it's desirable to gain as much distance from them as possible...

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:32.800
...that's what Reinhard thought.

00:20:34.690 --> 00:20:37.240
And his plan was exactly right.

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:40.200
While the Alliance patrol ships that rushed to the scene stayed there...

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:43.400
...he succeeded in escaping from
their possible search radar.

00:20:43.400 --> 00:20:48.000
Using route data collected from Helksheimer's ship, they found a group of small comets...

00:20:48.000 --> 00:20:51.000
...which seemed to have resulted from the disintegration of a larger comet...

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:53.160
...and hid his ships there
as a temporary refuge.

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:06.110
How is it going ?

00:21:06.110 --> 00:21:07.730
Without a doubt, this is it.

00:21:07.730 --> 00:21:09.050
But there's a problem.

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:13.550
While the Seffle particles
can be generated...

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:17.950
...I can't access the program 
that controls their directionality.

00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:19.340
It's protected ?

00:21:19.340 --> 00:21:20.470
Yes.

00:21:20.470 --> 00:21:22.790
The access code has been changed.

00:21:22.790 --> 00:21:27.830
But it can be deciphered if we get it home and take our time, can't it ?

00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:33.410
Um, yes, that's right, but we can
make more effective use of the time.

00:21:33.410 --> 00:21:38.330
If Helksheimer had been alive, he would have obviously known the access code.

00:21:38.330 --> 00:21:42.740
Since we couldn't secure his person, I at least need a keyword...

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:47.010
Come to think of it, isn't there 
anyone else who might know it ?

00:21:47.010 --> 00:21:48.150
Like the captain of this ship ?

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:54.710
The captain and other crew members seem to be merely hired help.

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:56.590
What about Helksheimer's family ?

00:21:56.590 --> 00:21:59.060
There's only one survivor.

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:00.330
Let me meet with him !

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:01.550
I would think it's useless, but...

00:22:02.800 --> 00:22:04.950
I'll decide whether it's useless or not.

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:18.950
Status ?

00:22:18.950 --> 00:22:19.900
Still sleeping.

00:22:20.570 --> 00:22:21.950
What's going on here ?

00:22:21.950 --> 00:22:23.460
Why only one guard ?

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:25.150
Isn't that too careless ?

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:36.490
Um, this is...?

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:37.770
Yes.

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:43.350
The only survivor is Lady
Margareta of the Helksheimers.

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:44.490
She's just a child !

00:22:47.900 --> 00:22:49.430
Who's there ?

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:52.800
As Reinhard unwittingly stated...

00:22:52.800 --> 00:22:56.110
...the return journey would be more
troublesome than their infiltration.

00:22:56.110 --> 00:23:01.620
Moreover, an adversity that no 
one expected lay ahead of him.

00:23:00.150 --> 00:23:03.600
To be continued

